{
  "section": "llm-direct-preference-optimization",
  "addedResources": [],
  "units": [
    {
      "id": "kl-regularized-optimal-policy",
      "title": "The KL-regularized objective and its optimal policy",
      "hours": 4,
      "target": [
        "derive"
      ],
      "topics": [
        "rl-10-kl-regularized-reward-maximization-objective",
        "rl-10-optimal-policy-form-under-a-reference-policy"
      ],
      "outcomes": [
        "Derive the optimal completion distribution for a fixed reward and reference policy.",
        "Explain how KL direction, reference support and the regularization coefficient constrain that solution."
      ],
      "why": "DPO starts with a reward-maximization problem, not a classification trick. Its closed-form solution tells you which reference and support assumptions the later loss inherits.",
      "next": [
        {
          "unit": "reinforcement-learning/llm-direct-preference-optimization/dpo-derivation",
          "note": "Invert the optimal-policy expression and eliminate the partition function."
        }
      ],
      "study": [
        {
          "resource": "rl10-rlhf-book-direct-alignment",
          "title": "The RLHF Book: the optimal RLHF solution",
          "url": "https://rlhfbook.com/c/08-direct-alignment.html#dpo-derivation",
          "locator": "Chapter 8, DPO Derivation: Deriving the Optimal RLHF Solution (page read 2026-10-14)",
          "depth": "Stop before Deriving DPO Objectives for BT Models. Track maximizers, not equality of the objective values after sign changes and division by the regularization coefficient."
        },
        {
          "resource": "rl10-direct-preference-optimization",
          "title": "DPO: optimal-policy derivation",
          "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.18290v3",
          "locator": "arXiv:2305.18290v3, Section 3 equation 3, Section 4 equation 4 and Appendix A.1"
        }
      ],
      "focus": [
        "For each prompt, maximize expected reward minus \\(\\beta D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(\\pi\\|\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}})\\), with \\(\\beta>0\\). The expectation defining KL is under the policy being optimized.",
        "The solution is \\(\\pi^*(y\\mid x)=\\pi_{\\mathrm{ref}}(y\\mid x)\\exp(r(x,y)/\\beta)/Z(x)\\). Require a finite partition function and optimize on reference support.",
        "Reuse Entropy, cross-entropy, KL divergence and mutual information and Constraints, KKT conditions and proximal methods. Here the new step is rewriting the reward objective as \\(\\beta\\log Z(x)-\\beta D_{\\mathrm{KL}}(\\pi\\|\\pi^*)\\)."
      ],
      "reproduce": "Closed-book, derive the solution by normalization and certify it with the KL rewrite. For one prompt and two completions, take reference probabilities \\((1/4,3/4)\\), rewards \\((\\log 3,0)\\) and \\(\\beta=1\\). Obtain \\(Z=3/2\\), \\(\\pi^*=(1/2,1/2)\\) and optimal objective \\(\\log(3/2)\\). Check in CPU PyTorch with `torch.float64`, natural logs and absolute tolerance \\(10^{-12}\\); no sampling or model is needed.",
      "traps": [
        "Reversing KL. A reference-to-policy penalty yields a different optimization problem.",
        "Reading the penalty coefficient as a hard KL budget. A finite-data DPO run does not attain a prescribed divergence simply by setting it.",
        "Giving positive mass to a completion with zero reference probability. Its policy-to-reference KL is infinite."
      ],
      "selfTest": [
        {
          "q": "Why does the partition function not change while optimizing the policy?",
          "a": "The reward, reference and regularization coefficient are fixed. The partition function depends on these and the prompt, not on the candidate policy."
        },
        {
          "q": "What happens as the regularization coefficient grows without bound for bounded rewards?",
          "a": "The exponential reward tilt approaches one, and the optimum approaches the reference distribution."
        },
        {
          "q": "Can a single sampled log-ratio be negative although KL is nonnegative?",
          "a": "Yes. Nonnegativity holds for the expectation under the policy, not for each completion's log-ratio."
        }
      ],
      "practice": [
        {
          "name": "TensorTonic: Score a KL-Regularized Policy Batch",
          "url": "https://www.tensortonic.com/problems/cs336-l15-kl-regularized-policy-objective",
          "locator": "Language Modeling from Scratch: `kl_regularized_policy_objective`; all three public cases",
          "note": "The free problem checks positive sampled divergence, a mixed masked batch and a fully masked response. It scores supplied log probabilities; it does not prove the optimal-policy formula or estimate KL from arbitrary offline preferences."
        }
      ],
      "stopWhen": "You derive and certify the two-completion optimum unaided and pass the named problem without clamping negative sampled divergences.",
      "fit": {
        "verdict": "kept",
        "note": "The book and paper provide the complete derivation and assumptions; the named free problem checks the sampled objective within four hours."
      }
    },
